\subsection{The [[--ai]] and [[--no-ai]] flags}

The AI-summary flags are a pair whose registration depends on the
environment: [[--ai]] can only deliver summaries when the optional [[llm]]
package is installed, so we probe for the package and register [[--ai]] only
when the probe succeeds.  We register [[--no-ai]] unconditionally, for two
reasons: scripts written on a machine with [[llm]] keep working on a machine
without it, and its help text is where we point the user to the optional
extra whose installation would enable [[--ai]].

Both flags write to the same destination, [[args.ai]], and argparse fills in
the default from the first action registered for that destination.  With the
conditional registration, the effective default would thus follow whichever
flag happens to exist.  An earlier version did exactly that: [[--no-ai]]
carried [[default=True]], so precisely on installations \emph{without} the
AI backend the analysis ran with AI enabled, injecting error messages about
the missing [[llm]] package into every generated report.  The default must
follow the capability, not the flag registration.  Both actions therefore
declare the same default, [[False]]: AI summaries are strictly opt-in
through [[--ai]], which only exists when the backend does, so the default
comes out right no matter which of the two actions argparse consults.

