\chapter{Quiz Management Commands}
\label{quiz-management}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

This chapter implements commands for creating, editing, and deleting quizzes.
Canvas provides two quiz systems with different APIs:
\begin{description}
\item[Classic Quizzes] The original quiz system, accessed via
  [[/api/v1/courses/:id/quizzes]]. The [[canvasapi]] library provides native
  support through [[course.create_quiz()]] and similar methods.
\item[New Quizzes] The modern quiz system (formerly Quizzes.Next), accessed via
  [[/api/quiz/v1/courses/:id/quizzes]]. This API requires direct HTTP requests
  since [[canvasapi]] does not provide native support.
\end{description}

The commands in this chapter support both systems, auto-detecting the quiz type
when editing existing quizzes and allowing explicit type selection when creating
new quizzes.


\section{Keeping the quiz listing cache honest}
\label{sec:quiz-listing-invalidation}

Creating, editing and deleting a quiz changes what a course contains; every
other quiz command begins by asking the course what it contains.  Between
the two sits a cache that only the askers knew about, and that asymmetry is
enough to break the export--create--edit workflow this chapter exists to
support.

\subsection{Why a write path has to clear a read cache}

[[fetch_all_quizzes]] reaches Canvas through [[course.get_quizzes()]] and
[[course.get_new_quizzes()]], and [[make_course_quizzes_cacheable]] has
wrapped both in [[CacheGetMethods]].  The wrapper gives every [[Course]]
object a dictionary of the quizzes it has seen---[[quiz_cache]] for Classic
Quizzes, [[new_quiz_cache]] for New Quizzes---and a timestamp recording when
it last asked for the \emph{complete} list, [[quiz_all_fetched]] and
[[new_quiz_all_fetched]].  As long as a timestamp stands, the plural method
does not call Canvas at all: it replays its dictionary.  And [[Course]]
objects are pickled to disk between runs, so the timestamp outlives the
process that set it.  The only thing that clears it on its own is the
[[QUIZ_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]] timer (\cref{sec:cache-ttl-constants}), three days
later.

[[create_command]] creates a quiz with a raw [[POST]], a path the wrapper
never sees.  The course's listing is then \emph{complete and wrong}: complete
according to [[new_quiz_all_fetched]], wrong in fact.  The
export--create--edit round trip documented above therefore fails on its last
step, seconds after its first:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz.json  # Created quiz (ID: 394105)
canvaslms quizzes edit -c datintro26 -f quiz.json --replace-items
# No quiz found with title: Git quiz: what you know already
\end{minted}
and it fails by reporting that a quiz whose ID we just printed does not
exist---a diagnosis that sends the reader looking in the wrong place, since
the same command with [[--no-cache]] finds the quiz immediately.

Leaving this to the TTL is no answer even in principle.  Three days is far
longer than the seconds separating two commands of a script, and shortening
the interval enough to cover this case would discard the cache for every
reader, including the many that never write anything.  A write is what makes
the listing wrong, it is the only event that knows this, and the process
performing it also knows exactly which course it wrote to.  Invalidating
there is both precise and immediate; a TTL can be neither.

\subsection{Two caches, two invalidations}

The chapter already contains a function with a similar name and a different
job, and the two must not be confused.  [[invalidate_quiz_items_cache]]
(\cref{quiz-items-cache}) drops [[quiz._items_cache]]: the \emph{questions}
of one quiz, fetched by [[export_new_quiz_items]] and held for minutes within
a single run.  It answers \enquote{what is in this quiz?}.  The cache we
clear here answers \enquote{which quizzes are in this course?}, lives on the
[[Course]] rather than the quiz, and is written to disk.  Replacing a quiz's
items leaves the course listing correct, and creating a quiz leaves every
other quiz's items correct, so neither call substitutes for the other.

Forgetting a listing means putting the [[Course]] back in the state it had
before anything was fetched: an empty dictionary and no \enquote{all
fetched} timestamp, which is exactly what the plural method treats as
\enquote{ask Canvas}.  We reset both quiz systems on every call, because
[[fetch_all_quizzes]] reads both and the caller cannot generally tell which
system a title, an ID or a module placement will end up matching.

The [[hasattr]] guards follow [[invalidate_module_items]] in
[[canvaslms.cli.modules]].  The four attributes exist only on classes that
[[make_course_quizzes_cacheable]] has wrapped, so on a plain [[canvasapi]]
[[Course]]---what a caller holds when the hacks were never installed---the
helper must be a silent no-op rather than an [[AttributeError]] raised right
after a successful write.

<<functions>>=
def invalidate_quiz_listing_cache(course):
  """Forget the cached quiz listing of `course` so the next access refetches.

  Clears both the Classic and the New Quizzes listing; safe to call on a
  course that was never wrapped for caching.
  """
  if hasattr(course, 'quiz_cache'):
    course.quiz_cache.clear()
  if hasattr(course, 'quiz_all_fetched'):
    course.quiz_all_fetched = None
  if hasattr(course, 'new_quiz_cache'):
    course.new_quiz_cache.clear()
  if hasattr(course, 'new_quiz_all_fetched'):
    course.new_quiz_all_fetched = None
@

Let us verify that a course whose listing has been fully fetched is returned
to the never-fetched state, and that a course without the caching attributes
survives the call.

<<test functions>>=
def cached_course(course_id=1):
  """A MagicMock course whose quiz listings are cached as fully fetched"""
  course = MagicMock()
  course.id = course_id
  course.quiz_cache = {11: ("classic quiz", {})}
  course.new_quiz_cache = {12: ("new quiz", {})}
  course.quiz_all_fetched = "some timestamp"
  course.new_quiz_all_fetched = "some timestamp"
  return course

def test_invalidate_quiz_listing_clears_both_quiz_systems():
  course = cached_course()

  quizzes.invalidate_quiz_listing_cache(course)

  assert course.quiz_cache == {}
  assert course.new_quiz_cache == {}
  assert course.quiz_all_fetched is None
  assert course.new_quiz_all_fetched is None

def test_invalidate_quiz_listing_ignores_uncached_course():
  quizzes.invalidate_quiz_listing_cache(object())
@

