<<functions>>=
def create_new_quiz(course, requester, quiz_params):
  """Creates a New Quiz via the New Quizzes API

  Args:
    course: Course object
    requester: Canvas API requester for direct HTTP calls
    quiz_params: Dictionary of quiz parameters, may include nested quiz_settings

  Returns:
    Dictionary with created quiz data, or None on failure
  """
  endpoint = f"courses/{course.id}/quizzes"

  # Build the request parameters, handling nested quiz_settings
  params = build_new_quiz_api_params(quiz_params)

  try:
    response = requester.request(
      method='POST',
      endpoint=endpoint,
      _url="new_quizzes",
      **params
    )
    data = response.json()

    return data
  except Exception as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Failed to create New Quiz: {e}")
    return None
@

The [[build_new_quiz_api_params]] function handles the conversion of our
nested dictionary structure into the flat parameter format required by the
Canvas API.  It recursively processes [[quiz_settings]] and its sub-structures.

Keys the function does not recognise become top-level quiz parameters.  That
default is right for [[title]] and [[due_at]], and wrong for exactly one key.

\subsubsection{The time limit is not a top-level field}
\label{sec:new-quiz-time-limit-params}

A New Quiz has no writable top-level [[time_limit]].  Sending
\begin{verbatim}
quiz[time_limit]=1800
\end{verbatim}
is accepted, answered with a success status, and changes nothing: the quiz
still reports [[has_time_limit: false]] afterwards (sandbox test, August
2026).  What governs the timer is a pair of settings one level down:
\begin{verbatim}
quiz[quiz_settings][has_time_limit]=true
quiz[quiz_settings][session_time_limit_in_seconds]=1800
\end{verbatim}

A silent no-op is the worst failure mode available here---the quiz opens
untimed and nothing in our output says so---so we translate [[time_limit]]
into that pair instead of letting the generic rule map it into a field
nobody reads.  Writing the pair by hand always worked; what did not work was
the [[time_limit]] our own help text advertises.

The two settings have to be written as a pair, because [[has_time_limit]] is
the switch and [[session_time_limit_in_seconds]] is only consulted when the
switch is on.  A [[time_limit]] of [[null]] or [[0]]---the two ways our file
formats spell \enquote{no time limit}---therefore turns the switch off and
sends no duration at all.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_time_limit_params(seconds):
  """Return the New Quizzes API parameters for a time limit

  `seconds` is the limit in seconds, the unit New Quizzes count in; a
  falsy value (null or 0) means the quiz has no time limit.
  """
  if not seconds:
    return {HAS_TIME_LIMIT_PARAM: False}

  return {HAS_TIME_LIMIT_PARAM: True, SESSION_TIME_LIMIT_PARAM: seconds}
<<constants>>=
HAS_TIME_LIMIT_PARAM = 'quiz[quiz_settings][has_time_limit]'
SESSION_TIME_LIMIT_PARAM = \
    'quiz[quiz_settings][session_time_limit_in_seconds]'
@

\subsubsection{Which statement wins when a file makes two}

A file written by [[quizzes export]] states the limit twice: once as the
friendly [[time_limit]], and once inside the [[quiz_settings]] Canvas
returned, as the pair itself.  Editing either and feeding the file back is a
reasonable thing to do, so the two statements can disagree, and something has
to choose between them.

We let [[time_limit]] win.  It is the key our documentation advertises, the
one an instructor is most likely to have edited, and the only one a
hand-written file usually carries; the pair is what an export happened to
bring along.  Choosing the other way would reinstate this very bug---an
edited [[time_limit]] with no effect---for everyone whose file came from
[[quizzes export]], which is the population most likely to hit it.  A file
that states only the pair is unaffected: with no [[time_limit]] key there is
nothing to override, so the hand-written workaround keeps working.

Neither statement is discarded quietly.  When a file makes both and they
disagree, we say which one we used; agreement, which is what an untouched
round trip produces, passes without a word.

<<functions>>=
def quiz_settings_time_limit(quiz_settings):
  """Return the time limit in seconds stated by a quiz_settings mapping

  Returns None when the mapping is absent, states no time limit, or
  states a zero-length one.
  """
  if not quiz_settings or not quiz_settings.get('has_time_limit'):
    return None

  return quiz_settings.get('session_time_limit_in_seconds') or None
@

Comparing the two statements means reading a duration back out of a
[[quiz_settings]] mapping, which is also how [[quizzes export]] and the
editor learn a New Quiz's time limit
(\cref{sec:reading-new-quiz-time-limit}).  The switch decides there too: a
mapping whose [[has_time_limit]] is false states \enquote{no limit} however
many seconds sit beside it, and Canvas does leave a stale [[0]] beside a
switched-off timer.

<<functions>>=
def build_new_quiz_api_params(quiz_params):
  """Converts quiz parameters to Canvas API format

  Handles nested structures like quiz_settings.multiple_attempts by
  flattening them into the format:
    quiz[quiz_settings][multiple_attempts][key]=value

  A top-level time_limit is translated into the quiz_settings pair the
  New Quizzes API reads, and overrides a time limit stated in an
  explicit quiz_settings.

  Args:
    quiz_params: Dictionary with quiz parameters, may include nested dicts

  Returns:
    Dictionary suitable for passing to requester.request()
  """
  params = {}

  for key, value in quiz_params.items():
    if key == 'time_limit':
      continue

    if value is None:
      continue

    if key == 'quiz_settings' and isinstance(value, dict):
      # Handle nested quiz_settings structure
      for settings_key, settings_value in value.items():
        if settings_value is None:
          continue

        if isinstance(settings_value, dict):
          # Handle doubly-nested structures like multiple_attempts, result_view_settings
          for nested_key, nested_value in settings_value.items():
            if nested_value is not None:
              param_key = f'quiz[quiz_settings][{settings_key}][{nested_key}]'
              params[param_key] = nested_value
        else:
          # Direct quiz_settings value (e.g., shuffle_answers)
          param_key = f'quiz[quiz_settings][{settings_key}]'
          params[param_key] = settings_value
    else:
      # Top-level quiz parameter
      params[f'quiz[{key}]'] = value

  <<translate the time limit into quiz settings parameters>>

  return params
@

The translation happens after the loop, not inside it, so that the outcome
does not depend on which key a dictionary happened to yield first.  The
duration is dropped before the pair is written rather than overwritten with
it, because clearing a limit writes no duration at all and would otherwise
leave the exported one standing beside a switched-off timer.

