\section{The [[quizzes edit]] subcommand}
\label{sec:quizzes-edit}

The [[quizzes edit]] command modifies an existing quiz's settings and
instructions. Like [[assignments edit]], it supports two workflows:
\begin{description}
\item[Interactive mode] (default) Opens your editor with the quiz content
  formatted as YAML front matter (settings) plus Markdown body (instructions).
  After editing, shows a preview and asks whether to accept, edit further, or
  discard.
\item[File mode] ([[-f]]) Reads content from a file for scripted workflows.
  Useful for batch updates or Git-based quiz management.
\end{description}

The command auto-detects whether the quiz is a New Quiz or Classic Quiz and
uses the appropriate API. A key challenge is that the two quiz systems use
different field names and units:
\begin{description}
\item[Instructions] New Quizzes use [[instructions]], Classic Quizzes use
  [[description]].
\item[Time limit] New Quizzes count seconds, Classic Quizzes count minutes.
\end{description}

The time limit forces every representation we generate to pick a unit, and our
two representations pick differently.  The front matter we hand to the editor
is written for a human who thinks in minutes, so a New Quiz value is divided
on the way in and multiplied back on the way out.  The JSON (and equivalent
YAML) representation is the interchange format shared with [[quizzes export]]
and [[quizzes create]]; there the value keeps Canvas's own unit, so that
exporting a quiz and feeding the untouched file straight back is a no-op.

Keeping the two conventions apart matters more than which one we picked.  A
conversion attached to the API call rather than to the representation cannot
tell the representations apart, so it multiplies values that were never
divided: a 30-minute quiz exported as [[1800]] would come back as [[108000]]
seconds---30 hours---and each further round trip would multiply again.  We
therefore convert where the front matter is built and consumed
(\cref{sec:front-matter-time-limit}) and let the shared API-parameter
conversion pass the value through untouched.

Untouched means unrescaled, not unmoved.  A New Quiz has no top-level time
limit for those seconds to land in, so the shared conversion re-addresses
them as the [[quiz_settings]] pair Canvas actually reads
(\cref{sec:new-quiz-time-limit-params}).  The unit is what must survive the
journey; the address is ours to get right.


\subsection{Quiz schema for editing}

We define a schema for quiz properties, similar to [[ASSIGNMENT_SCHEMA]] in
[[content.nw]]. This schema describes all editable properties and provides
defaults for the editor template.

\paragraph{Field selection rationale.}
We include fields that instructors commonly need to adjust:
\begin{description}
\item[Identity and content] [[title]], [[id]] (read-only, for identification).
\item[Timing] [[time_limit]], [[due_at]], [[unlock_at]], [[lock_at]]---the most
  frequently edited settings when reusing quizzes across semesters.
\item[Scoring] [[points_possible]], [[allowed_attempts]]---controls grading behavior.
\item[Presentation] [[shuffle_questions]], [[shuffle_answers]],
  [[one_question_at_a_time]], [[cant_go_back]]---anti-cheating and UX options.
\item[Feedback] [[show_correct_answers]], [[hide_results]]---controls what
  students see after submission.
\item[Access control] [[access_code]], [[published]]---security and visibility.
\item[Quiz type] [[quiz_type]]---distinguishes graded quizzes from surveys.
\end{description}
We deliberately exclude fields like [[ip_filter]] (rarely used),
[[scoring_policy]] (complex interactions with other settings), and
[[assignment_group_id]] (better managed through the assignments command).

