\subsection{File formats for quiz editing}
\label{sec:quiz-file-formats}

\subsection{Updating module membership}

When a quiz is edited from a file, the front matter may include a [[modules]]
field. We interpret this the same way as [[assignments edit]]:
\begin{description}
\item[Absent] Leave module membership unchanged.
\item[Empty list] Remove the quiz from all modules.
\item[List of specs] Ensure the quiz appears in every matching module and is
  removed from non-matching modules; a spec with a position also moves the
  quiz there and one with a completion requirement sets it (e.g.
  [[must_submit]] for a quiz that unlocks a module), a plain regex leaves
  both alone (\cref{sec:module-positions,sec:completion-requirements}).
\end{description}

New Quizzes are implemented as assignments, and the module item type Canvas uses
is [[Assignment]], with the assignment ID. Classic Quizzes are listed in
modules as items of type [[Quiz]], with the quiz ID. The two therefore need
different item types when talking to the modules helpers; [[quiz_module_item]]
makes that choice in one place.

We centralize this logic in a helper function so the file-based and
interactive edit paths, and [[quizzes create]], behave identically.
The lower-level [[apply_quiz_module_specs]] takes the course and the item ID
rather than a quiz object, because [[quizzes create]] only has the ID Canvas
returned when the quiz was made; [[update_quiz_module_membership]] computes
the ID from a quiz object for the edit paths.

The [[quizzes edit]] command supports three file formats, auto-detected from
the file extension and verified against the content:
\begin{description}
\item[JSON format] ([[.json]]) Full quiz structure with [[settings]] and
  optional [[items]].  This is the same format used by [[quizzes export]] and
  [[quizzes create]], enabling a round-trip workflow.
\item[YAML format] ([[.yaml]], [[.yml]]) Same structure as JSON but in YAML
  syntax.  More readable for hand-editing.
\item[Front matter format] ([[.md]]) YAML front matter with Markdown body for
  instructions.  This is the default interactive editing format, focused on
  editing settings and instructions without touching questions.
\end{description}

The formats agree on field names but not on the unit of [[time_limit]] for a
New Quiz: the front matter states it in minutes, JSON and YAML state it in
seconds.  The split follows from what each format is for.  Front matter is
typed by hand, so it uses the unit instructors think in; JSON and YAML are
what [[quizzes export]] wrote, so they must repeat what Canvas reported or the
export/edit round trip would not be idempotent.

The format is detected by file extension and verified by checking the first
characters of the file:
\begin{itemize}
\item JSON files start with [[{]]
\item YAML full format starts with [[quiz_type:]] or [[settings:]]
\item Front matter format starts with [[---]]
\end{itemize}

<<functions>>=
def quiz_module_item(quiz):
  """Return (item_type, item_id) identifying `quiz` as a module item."""
  if is_new_quiz(quiz):
    return 'Assignment', int(quiz.id)
  return 'Quiz', quiz.id

def apply_quiz_module_specs(course, item_type, item_id, module_specs):
  """Place the quiz appearing as module item (item_type, item_id)."""
  added, removed, moved, required = modules.update_item_modules(
      course, item_type, item_id, module_specs)
  modules.print_module_changes(added, removed, moved, required)

def update_quiz_module_membership(quiz, module_specs):
  """Update module membership for a quiz based on module specs"""
  item_type, item_id = quiz_module_item(quiz)
  apply_quiz_module_specs(quiz.course, item_type, item_id, module_specs)
