\subsection{Processing the edit command}
\label{sec:quiz-edit-processing}

The edit command has two modes: interactive (default) and file-based.
In interactive mode, we open the editor for each matching quiz.
In file mode, we read the content from the specified file.

Both modes end by forgetting the cached listings, because an edit changes
precisely the fields the listing is searched by---a renamed quiz is matched
under its old title until the cache is refetched, and printed under it by
[[quizzes list]].  Both modes leave the selection in [[quiz_list]], so one
chunk after the branch covers them; the aborts inside the branches return
before reaching it, which is right, since an abort changed nothing.

<<functions>>=
def edit_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Edits quiz settings and instructions"""
  requester = canvas._Canvas__requester

  if args.file:
    <<edit quiz from file>>
  else:
    if not getattr(args, 'assignment', None):
      canvaslms.cli.err(1, "-a/--assignment is required unless -f/--file is used")
    quiz_list = process_quiz_option(canvas, args)
    <<edit quiz interactively>>

  <<forget the cached quiz listings of the edited courses>>
@

A selection can span several courses, and usually spans several quizzes of
the same one.  We invalidate per course rather than per quiz because the
listing is a property of the course, not of the quiz that happened to lead us
to it; deduplicating by course ID says that in the code as well.  A quiz
reaches us from [[filter_quizzes]] with its course attached, but we ask
rather than assume, so that a quiz assembled some other way cannot turn a
successful edit into an [[AttributeError]].

<<forget the cached quiz listings of the edited courses>>=
invalidated_courses = set()
for quiz in quiz_list:
  course = getattr(quiz, 'course', None)
  if course is None or course.id in invalidated_courses:
    continue
  invalidated_courses.add(course.id)
  invalidate_quiz_listing_cache(course)
