The settings are applied using [[apply_quiz_edit]], to which we hand the body
[[read_quiz_from_file]] has already derived.  What that function had to be
taught---which key of a settings dictionary carries the body---is worth
stating carefully, because getting it wrong loses text silently.

\paragraph{The export--edit round-trip invariant.}
The file is normally one that [[quizzes export]] wrote, so the two commands
have to agree about where the body lives.  We state that agreement as an
invariant:
\begin{quote}
  A no-op round trip is a no-op on Canvas: [[quizzes export -a Q > q.json]]
  followed by [[quizzes edit -f q.json]], with nothing edited in between,
  must leave the quiz exactly as it was.
\end{quote}

The invariant does not hold by itself, because the two quiz systems name the
body differently: [[export_full_new_quiz]] writes it under [[instructions]],
[[export_full_classic_quiz]] under [[description]].  Looking only under
[[instructions]] finds nothing in a Classic file and yields an empty body,
which [[apply_quiz_edit]] then writes over the description the file
carried---the round trip erasing the very text it had just exported.  Nothing
in the output says so; the quiz simply loses its instructions.

Reading whichever key the file uses restores the invariant.  We put that
choice in one function, so that every site deriving a body from a settings
dictionary derives it the same way:

<<functions>>=
def quiz_body_from_settings(settings):
  """Return the body carried by a quiz settings dictionary

  New Quiz exports store the body under 'instructions', Classic Quiz
  exports under 'description'.  Returns the empty string if the settings
  carry neither.
  """
  return settings.get('instructions') or settings.get('description') or ''
@

One could instead unify the two formats---write the body under a single key
regardless of quiz system---and no reader function would be needed.
We deliberately don't.
The JSON and YAML files mirror what the respective Canvas API returns, so
Canvas's own API documentation describes our files for free; a renamed key
would cost us that, break every already-exported file, and reintroduce the
shape of this very bug with the roles reversed, since [[export]],
[[create]] and the API paths would each need a translation step that all
sites must agree on.
The unification the reader actually wants exists at the human-facing level
already: in the editor representation, [[extract_quiz_attributes]] presents
both quiz systems' bodies as [[instructions]] and the Markdown body below
the front matter.
So the machine format stays faithful to each API, and the reconciliation
happens exactly once, here, in code.

Exactly two sites hold a settings dictionary and need its body, and both ask
this function: [[_parse_quiz_full_format]] (\cref{sec:quiz-file-formats}),
which answers while reading the file so that the loop below can take the body
as given, and [[apply_quiz_from_dict]], which gets its dictionary straight from
the interactive JSON editor and never sees a file at all.  The loop is thus
free of the question, and a third answer to it cannot quietly appear.

Let's verify that both spellings are found:

<<test functions>>=
def test_quiz_body_from_settings_reads_either_key():
  body = '<p>Read carefully.</p>'

  assert quizzes.quiz_body_from_settings({'description': body}) == body
  assert quizzes.quiz_body_from_settings({'instructions': body}) == body
  assert quizzes.quiz_body_from_settings({'title': 'Exam'}) == ''
@

The formats also disagree about the unit of [[time_limit]], and the
disagreement resolves per quiz rather than per file: a front-matter time limit
needs converting only when the quiz it targets is a New Quiz.  We therefore
convert inside the loop, where the quiz type is known, and leave the JSON and
YAML settings alone because they already carry Canvas's units
(\cref{sec:front-matter-time-limit}).

