\subsection{Extracting quiz attributes}

To populate the editor with current quiz values, we extract attributes from
the quiz object. We use [[QUIZ_SCHEMA]] to determine which attributes to
extract, and also include [[instructions]] separately (since it becomes the
body content, not a YAML attribute).

For New Quizzes, we also extract [[quiz_settings]] which contains the
important [[multiple_attempts]] and [[result_view_settings]] structures.

This function is where the front-matter unit convention starts: a New Quiz
time limit arrives from Canvas in seconds and leaves here in minutes.  A zero
or missing limit becomes [[None]], because Canvas expresses \enquote{no time
limit} both ways and the editor should show the absence rather than a
misleading [[0]].

\paragraph{Where a New Quiz keeps its time limit.}
\label{sec:reading-new-quiz-time-limit}
Those seconds do not arrive in the attribute named after them.  A New Quiz
object reports [[time_limit]] as [[null]] even when its timer is running---the
mirror image of the field being ignored on the way in
(\cref{sec:new-quiz-time-limit-params})---and the value lives in
[[quiz_settings]] in both directions.  We still read the attribute first, so
that an object which does carry a limit is believed, and fall back to the
settings when it does not.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_time_limit(quiz, quiz_settings):
  """Return a New Quiz's time limit in seconds, or None

  Canvas reports the limit in `quiz_settings`; the quiz object's own
  time_limit attribute is null for New Quizzes, and is consulted first
  only so that an object which does carry one is believed.
  """
  return (getattr(quiz, 'time_limit', None)
          or quiz_settings_time_limit(quiz_settings))
@

The fallback is what moves the settings fetch ahead of the attribute loop.
Fetching needs a requester, and callers without one---the front-matter tests,
any caller holding only a quiz object---simply get no settings and fall back
to nothing, exactly as the [[if requester]] guard at the old call site did.
Making a missing requester the fetching function's own business
(\cref{sec:fetching-new-quiz-settings}) keeps its three callers from
repeating that condition.

<<functions>>=
def extract_quiz_attributes(quiz, requester=None):
  """Extract editable attributes from a quiz object

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object (New Quiz or Classic Quiz)
    requester: Canvas API requester (needed for New Quiz settings)

  Returns:
    Dictionary of attributes matching QUIZ_SCHEMA, plus 'instructions'
    and 'quiz_settings' (for New Quizzes)
  """
  attrs = {}
  is_new = is_new_quiz(quiz)
  quiz_settings = fetch_new_quiz_settings(quiz, requester) if is_new else None

  for key, spec in QUIZ_SCHEMA.items():
    canvas_attr = spec.get('canvas_attr', key)
    value = getattr(quiz, canvas_attr, spec.get('default'))

    if key == 'time_limit' and is_new:
      seconds = new_quiz_time_limit(quiz, quiz_settings)
      value = seconds // SECONDS_PER_MINUTE if seconds else None

    if value is not None:
      attrs[key] = value

  # Add instructions (not in schema, but needed for content_attr)
  if is_new:
    attrs['instructions'] = getattr(quiz, 'instructions', '') or ''
    if quiz_settings:
      attrs['quiz_settings'] = quiz_settings
  else:
    attrs['instructions'] = getattr(quiz, 'description', '') or ''

  return attrs
<<constants>>=
SECONDS_PER_MINUTE = 60
@

Let's verify that the editor is shown the limit Canvas actually keeps---a
quiz whose own attribute says nothing, and settings that say half an hour:

