\paragraph{Fetching New Quiz settings.}
\label{sec:fetching-new-quiz-settings}
The New Quizzes API returns [[quiz_settings]] as part of the quiz object,
but the [[canvasapi]] library may not expose all fields.  We make a direct
API call to get the complete settings.

A caller without a requester has no way to ask, which is a normal state
rather than a failure: the settings are simply unavailable.  Answering
[[None]] up front keeps that case from reaching the request and being
reported as a fetch failure the user can do nothing about.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_new_quiz_settings(quiz, requester):
  """Fetch quiz_settings from the New Quizzes API

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object (must have .id and .course attributes)
    requester: Canvas API requester, or None when unavailable

  Returns:
    Dictionary with quiz_settings, or None if unavailable
  """
  if requester is None:
    return None

  try:
    endpoint = f"courses/{quiz.course.id}/quizzes/{quiz.id}"
    response = requester.request(
      method='GET',
      endpoint=endpoint,
      _url="new_quizzes"
    )
    data = response.json()
    return data.get('quiz_settings', None)
  except Exception as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Failed to fetch New Quiz settings: {e}")
    return None
@


\subsection{Restoring the time limit unit}
\label{sec:front-matter-time-limit}

Everything downstream of the editor---the confirm-and-edit loop,
[[apply_quiz_edit]], the Canvas API---speaks seconds again, so the division
[[extract_quiz_attributes]] performed has to be undone before the attributes
leave the front-matter world.  The same undoing applies to a hand-written
[[.md]] file, which uses that same representation.

We undo it in a function of its own rather than inside [[apply_quiz_edit]]
because [[apply_quiz_edit]] is shared with the JSON paths, whose attributes
were never divided.  Provenance is the property that decides whether to
multiply, and a sink that receives dictionaries from three callers cannot
observe it; a function called once per front-matter ingress makes it visible
in the caller list.

The conversion is a no-op for Classic Quizzes, whose front matter and API
agree on minutes, and for a missing or unlimited time limit.  In those cases
we hand back the very dictionary we were given; otherwise we copy it, so that
a caller which keeps using its own attributes---as the interactive path does
for [[modules]]---never finds them silently rewritten.

<<functions>>=
def convert_front_matter_units(attributes, is_new):
  """Return quiz attributes with time_limit in the units Canvas expects

  The YAML front matter states a New Quiz time limit in minutes, whereas
  the New Quizzes API states it in seconds.  Classic Quizzes use minutes
  on both sides, so their attributes are returned unchanged.

  Args:
    attributes: Dictionary of attributes from YAML front matter
    is_new: True if this is a New Quiz

  Returns:
    The original dictionary, or a copy with time_limit converted
  """
  if not is_new or not attributes.get('time_limit'):
    return attributes

  converted = dict(attributes)
  converted['time_limit'] = attributes['time_limit'] * SECONDS_PER_MINUTE
  return converted
@

Let's verify that a New Quiz limit is scaled, that a Classic one is not, and
that the caller's dictionary survives the call untouched:

