\subsection{Applying quiz edits}

After editing, we need to translate the attributes back to API format and
call the appropriate update function. The key challenges are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Using [[instructions]] vs [[description]] for the body
\item Handling HTML conversion for Markdown input
\end{itemize}

Note what is \emph{not} on that list: unit conversion.  [[apply_quiz_edit]] is
the common sink for all three edit paths---front matter, JSON/YAML files, and
[[--full-json]]---and receives attributes in whichever units its caller
produced.  Only the front-matter callers convert; here [[time_limit]] is
already in Canvas's units and travels through unchanged.

<<functions>>=
def apply_quiz_edit(quiz, attributes, body, requester, html_mode=False):
  """Apply edited attributes and body to a quiz

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object to update
    attributes: Dictionary of attributes from YAML front matter
    body: Instructions/description content
    requester: Canvas API requester
    html_mode: If True, body is already HTML

  Returns:
    True on success, False on failure
  """
  # Convert body to HTML if needed
  if html_mode:
    html_body = body
  else:
    if body and body.strip():
      html_body = pypandoc.convert_text(body, 'html', format='md')
    else:
      html_body = ''

  # Build API parameters
  quiz_params = quiz_attributes_to_api_params(
    attributes, is_new_quiz(quiz), html_body
  )

  # Update based on quiz type
  if is_new_quiz(quiz):
    return update_new_quiz(quiz.course, quiz.id, requester, quiz_params)
  else:
    return update_classic_quiz(quiz, quiz_params)
@

\paragraph{From attributes to API parameters.}
The body reaches Canvas through exactly one channel, the [[html_body]]
argument.  The attribute loop therefore skips \emph{both} of its spellings,
and the trailing assignment writes it back under the name the quiz system
expects.

Letting [[description]] through the loop instead would put one field in two
places with the last write winning, and that is precisely how the round trip
came to erase quiz text: a caller that had derived the body correctly and one
that had derived nothing produced the same parameters, so whether the
description survived depended on statement order rather than on any decision.
With a single channel the two cases are distinguishable, and preserving the
body becomes the caller's job---which is what
[[quiz_body_from_settings]] discharges.

We still send an empty body rather than omitting the key, so that clearing
the Markdown body in the editor clears the description on Canvas.

Three keys in a settings mapping are ours rather than Canvas's: [[id]] and
[[regex]] identify the quiz for round trips (\cref{sec:quizzes-create} and
the file-based edit path), and [[modules]] is an instruction to the modules
helpers.
Both the edit path here and [[quizzes create]] must drop them before building
API parameters, so the list is named once.
<<constants>>=
CANVASLMS_SETTINGS_KEYS = ('id', 'regex', 'modules')
@

<<functions>>=
def quiz_attributes_to_api_params(attributes, is_new, html_body):
  """Convert schema attributes to Canvas API parameters

  Values are passed through in the units they arrive in; time_limit must
  already be in the units Canvas expects (seconds for New Quizzes, minutes
  for Classic Quizzes).

  Args:
    attributes: Dictionary of attributes from YAML
    is_new: True if this is a New Quiz
    html_body: HTML content for instructions/description

  Returns:
    Dictionary suitable for Canvas API (nested for New Quizzes)
  """
  params = {}

  for key, value in attributes.items():
    if key in CANVASLMS_SETTINGS_KEYS:
      continue

    if value is None:
      continue

    # Skip quiz_type for New Quizzes: New Quizzes are always graded assignments
    # and don't support the Classic Quiz distinction between assignment,
    # practice_quiz, graded_survey, and survey types.
    if key == 'quiz_type' and is_new:
      continue

    # Skip hide_results for New Quizzes: result visibility is controlled
    # through quiz_settings.result_view_settings, not this parameter.
    if key == 'hide_results' and is_new:
      continue

    # Pass through quiz_settings as-is for New Quizzes
    if key == 'quiz_settings' and is_new:
      params['quiz_settings'] = value
      continue

    # Skip both spellings of the body - it arrives via html_body
    if key in ('instructions', 'description'):
      continue

    params[key] = value

  # Add body with appropriate field name (include even if empty to allow clearing)
  params['instructions' if is_new else 'description'] = html_body

  return params
@

Let's verify the round trip through both quiz systems, and that an explicitly
