%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\chapter{Viewing Quiz Content}
\label{quiz-view}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

When preparing for a course or reviewing quiz content, instructors often need to
see the full content of a quiz---its instructions, questions, and answer choices.
While Canvas provides a web interface for this, it requires many clicks and is
not suitable for quick review or comparison between quizzes.

The [[quizzes view]] command provides a way to view quiz content directly in the
terminal. It renders the quiz as markdown, which can be:
\begin{itemize}
\item Displayed with rich formatting and a pager when output goes to a TTY
\item Output as plain markdown when piped to a file or another command
\end{itemize}

This design follows the Unix philosophy: when output is interactive, provide a
nice user experience; when output is piped, provide raw data for further
processing.


\section{The [[quizzes view]] subcommand}

The [[view]] command takes course and quiz selection options and displays the
full quiz content. If multiple quizzes match the pattern, all are displayed
sequentially.

<<add quizzes view command to subp>>=
view_parser = subp.add_parser("view",
    help="View quiz content (instructions and questions)",
    description="""Displays the full content of a quiz including instructions,
questions, and answer choices. For instructors, correct answers are marked.

Output format depends on terminal:
- TTY: Rich markdown rendering with pager support
- Pipe: Plain markdown for further processing""")

view_parser.set_defaults(func=view_command)

try:
  courses.add_course_option(view_parser, required=True)
except argparse.ArgumentError:
  pass

add_quiz_option(view_parser, required=True)
@

<<functions>>=
def add_view_command(subp):
  """Adds the view subcommand to the quizzes command group"""
  <<add quizzes view command to subp>>
@


\section{Processing the view command}

The view command processes each matching quiz and formats it as markdown.
We handle both New Quizzes and Classic Quizzes, using the appropriate API
for each type.

<<functions>>=
def view_command(config, canvas, args):
  """View quiz content including questions and answers"""
  quiz_list = process_quiz_option(canvas, args)
  requester = canvas._Canvas__requester

  output_parts = []

  for quiz in quiz_list:
    course = quiz.course if hasattr(quiz, 'course') else None
    if course is None:
      # Try to get course from quiz attributes
      course_id = getattr(quiz, 'course_id', None)
      if course_id:
        course = canvas.get_course(course_id)

    if is_new_quiz(quiz):
      markdown = format_new_quiz_as_markdown(quiz, course, requester)
    else:
      markdown = format_classic_quiz_as_markdown(quiz)

    output_parts.append(markdown)

  # Join all quizzes with a separator
  full_output = "\n\n---\n\n".join(output_parts)

  <<display quiz output respecting terminal type>>
@


\section{Rendering output}

We reuse the pattern established in the [[analyse]] command: when output goes
to a TTY, we use rich to render markdown with a pager; otherwise, we output
plain markdown for piping.

<<display quiz output respecting terminal type>>=
console = Console()
md = Markdown(full_output)

if sys.stdout.isatty():
  # Output to terminal with pager
  pager = ""
  if "MANPAGER" in os.environ:
    pager = os.environ["MANPAGER"]
  elif "PAGER" in os.environ:
    pager = os.environ["PAGER"]

  styles = False
  if "less" in pager and ("-R" in pager or "-r" in pager):
    styles = True

  with console.pager(styles=styles):
    console.print(md)
else:
  # Piped to file, output plain markdown
  print(full_output)
@


\section{Formatting New Quizzes}

New Quizzes store their content differently from Classic Quizzes. We use the
[[export_new_quiz_items]] function to fetch the full item data, then format
each item according to its type.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_as_markdown(quiz, course, requester):
  """Format a New Quiz as markdown

  Args:
    quiz: NewQuiz object
    course: Course object
    requester: Canvas API requester

  Returns:
    Markdown string with quiz content
  """
  lines = []

  # Quiz header
  title = getattr(quiz, 'title', 'Untitled Quiz')
  lines.append(f"# {title}")
  lines.append("")

  # Quiz metadata
  points = getattr(quiz, 'points_possible', None)
  if points:
    lines.append(f"**Points:** {points}")

  quiz_settings = fetch_new_quiz_settings(quiz, requester)
  time_limit = new_quiz_time_limit(quiz, quiz_settings)
  if time_limit:
    lines.append(f"**Time limit:** {format_time_limit(time_limit)}")

  if points or time_limit:
    lines.append("")

  # Instructions
  instructions = getattr(quiz, 'instructions', None) or getattr(quiz, 'description', None)
  if instructions:
    lines.append("## Instructions")
    lines.append("")
    # Convert HTML to plain text (basic cleanup)
    clean_instructions = html_to_markdown(instructions)
    lines.append(clean_instructions)
    lines.append("")

  # Fetch and format questions
  try:
    export_data = export_new_quiz_items(quiz, requester, include_banks=True)
    items = export_data.get('items', [])

    if items:
      lines.append("## Questions")
      lines.append("")

      for i, item in enumerate(items, 1):
        item_markdown = format_new_quiz_item(item, i)
        lines.append(item_markdown)
        lines.append("")

  except Exception as e:
    lines.append(f"*Could not fetch questions: {e}*")

  return "\n".join(lines)
@


\subsection{Reporting the time limit in a unit that is true}

The Classic formatter below prints a quiz's time limit followed by the word
[[minutes]], and it is right to: a Classic Quiz counts minutes.  Copying that
line into this formatter---which is how it got here---produced a sentence
that was wrong twice over.  New Quizzes count seconds, so a half-hour quiz
was announced as \enquote{1800 minutes}; and the seconds are not in the
attribute the line read (\cref{sec:reading-new-quiz-time-limit}), so in
practice the line never appeared at all.  A viewer that shows nothing and a
viewer that shows thirty hours are both worse than one that says thirty
minutes.

Reading the limit is the same problem the export and the editor have, and it
gets the same answer: the [[quiz_settings]] Canvas keeps it in.  That costs
one more request per quiz viewed, beside the request the formatter already
makes for the items---a cheap price for a metadata line that is otherwise
guaranteed to be either absent or false.

Rendering it is a choice this formatter has to make on its own.  We report
minutes, the unit the Classic line uses and the unit an instructor sets a
quiz in, so that two quizzes viewed side by side can be compared at all.  A
limit that is not a whole number of minutes is reported in seconds instead:
[[90]] seconds rendered as \enquote{1 minutes} would be the original bug in
smaller print, and in a viewer a number nobody can act on beats a number that
misleads.

<<functions>>=
def format_time_limit(seconds):
  """Return a time limit of `seconds` seconds as a human-readable string

  Whole minutes are reported in minutes, the unit quizzes are set in;
  anything else is reported in seconds rather than rounded.
  """
  minutes, remainder = divmod(seconds, SECONDS_PER_MINUTE)

  if minutes and not remainder:
    return f"{minutes} minutes"

  return f"{seconds} seconds"
@

Let's verify that a half-hour quiz is announced as half an hour, and that a
limit falling between minutes keeps its own unit:

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_time_limit_reports_whole_minutes_in_minutes():
  assert quizzes.format_time_limit(1800) == '30 minutes'


def test_format_time_limit_reports_a_partial_minute_in_seconds():
  assert quizzes.format_time_limit(90) == '90 seconds'
  assert quizzes.format_time_limit(45) == '45 seconds'
@

We can walk the whole path too, from Canvas's settings to the line a reader
sees: a quiz whose own [[time_limit]] is null, and settings that say
[[1800]], must be viewed as a half-hour quiz rather than as nothing or as
thirty hours.

<<test functions>>=
def test_view_reports_a_new_quiz_time_limit_in_minutes():
  class NewQuiz:
    pass

  quiz = NewQuiz()
  quiz.id = 42
  quiz.title = 'Midterm'
  quiz.time_limit = None
  quiz.course = MagicMock(id=1)

  requester = MagicMock()
  requester.request.return_value.json.return_value = {
    'quiz_settings': {'has_time_limit': True,
                      'session_time_limit_in_seconds': 1800}}

  markdown = quizzes.format_new_quiz_as_markdown(quiz, quiz.course, requester)

  assert '**Time limit:** 30 minutes' in markdown
  assert '1800' not in markdown
@


\subsection{Formatting individual New Quiz items}

New Quiz items come in several types: regular questions, stimuli (reading
passages), and bank references. We handle each appropriately.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_item(item, number):
  """Format a single New Quiz item as markdown

  Args:
    item: Item dictionary from export_new_quiz_items
    number: Question number for display

  Returns:
    Markdown string for the item
  """
  lines = []
  entry_type = item.get('entry_type', 'Item')
  entry = item.get('entry', {})
  points = item.get('points_possible', 0)

  # Handle bank references (without embedded question content)
  # 'BankEntry (embedded)' contains actual question data with 'item_body',
  # while pure 'Bank' references only have bank metadata (title, entry_count)
  is_bank_reference = entry_type in ('Bank', 'BankEntry') and 'item_body' not in entry
  if is_bank_reference:
    bank_ref = item.get('bank_reference', {})
    # Fall back to entry for bank metadata if bank_reference not available
    if not bank_ref:
      bank_ref = entry
    bank_title = bank_ref.get('title', 'Unknown Bank')
    sample_num = bank_ref.get('sample_num', item.get('properties', {}).get('sample_num', '1'))
    entry_count = bank_ref.get('entry_count', bank_ref.get('item_entry_count', '?'))
    lines.append(f"### Question {number} (from Item Bank)")
    lines.append(f"*Draws {sample_num} random question(s) from: {bank_title}*")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(f"*Bank contains {entry_count} questions. Actual content varies per student.*")
    return "\n".join(lines)

  # Handle stimulus (reading passage)
  if entry_type == 'Stimulus':
    title = entry.get('title', 'Reading Passage')
    body = entry.get('body', '')
    lines.append(f"### {title}")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(html_to_markdown(body))
    return "\n".join(lines)

  # Regular question item
  interaction_type = entry.get('interaction_type_slug', 'unknown')
  title = entry.get('title', f'Question {number}')
  body = entry.get('item_body', '')

  lines.append(f"### Question {number}: {title}")
  if points:
    lines.append(f"*({points} points)*")
  lines.append("")
  lines.append(html_to_markdown(body))
  lines.append("")

  # Format answers based on question type
  <<format new quiz answer choices>>

  return "\n".join(lines)
@

Different question types have different answer structures.
Canvas New Quizzes support many interaction types, each with its own data format.
We use helper functions to keep the formatting logic modular and testable.

<<format new quiz answer choices>>=
interaction_data = entry.get('interaction_data', {})
scoring_data = entry.get('scoring_data', {})

format_new_quiz_answers(lines, interaction_type, interaction_data, scoring_data)
@

The [[format_new_quiz_answers]] function dispatches to type-specific formatters.
This keeps the main formatting function focused on structure while delegating
answer rendering to specialized helpers.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_answers(lines, interaction_type, interaction_data, scoring_data):
  """Format answer choices for a New Quiz item

  Args:
    lines: List to append formatted lines to
    interaction_type: The question type (choice, true-false, etc.)
    interaction_data: Question-specific data (choices, stems, etc.)
    scoring_data: Correct answer information
  """
  if interaction_type in ('choice', 'multi-answer'):
    format_new_quiz_choice_answers(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data)
  elif interaction_type == 'ordering':
    format_new_quiz_ordering(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data)
  elif interaction_type == 'true-false':
    format_new_quiz_true_false(lines, scoring_data)
  elif interaction_type == 'matching':
    format_new_quiz_matching(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data)
  elif interaction_type in ('essay', 'rich-text'):
    lines.append("*(Essay response)*")
  elif interaction_type == 'numeric':
    format_new_quiz_numeric(lines, scoring_data)
  elif interaction_type == 'rich-fill-blank':
    format_new_quiz_fill_blank(lines, scoring_data)
  else:
    lines.append(f"*(Question type: {interaction_type})*")
@

\paragraph{Two structures behind one [[choices]] field.}
Three of the types above---[[choice]], [[multi-answer]] and [[ordering]]---keep
their alternatives in the [[choices]] field of [[interaction_data]], but not in
the same structure.  A choice or multi-answer question sends a \emph{list} of
choice objects, an ordering question a \emph{mapping} whose keys are the choice
identifiers:
\begin{minted}{json}
{"choices": [{"id": "uuid-1", "item_body": "<p>5</p>"}]}
{"choices": {"ord-1": {"id": "ord-1", "item_body": "<p>5</p>"}}}
\end{minted}
The export and import sides meet the same split and must branch on it
(\cref{sec:cleaning-export}), for the reason that bites here as well:
iterating a mapping yields its \emph{keys}, so code written for the list walks
over the strings [["ord-1"]], [["ord-2"]],~\dots\ and asks each of them for a
field that a string has no way to answer.

We would rather not have that structural test scattered over one renderer per
question type, so we normalize once---to the pairs that both structures
describe underneath, an identifier and the choice it names.  Delivery order is
what a list carries and all a mapping can offer, so we keep it in both cases;
the renderer that needs another order has an answer key to build it from.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_choice_pairs(choices):
  """Returns (choice id, choice) pairs for either choice structure

  Accepts the list of choice objects that a choice or multi-answer
  question carries as well as the id-keyed mapping of an ordering
  question, preserving in both cases the order Canvas delivered.
  """
  if isinstance(choices, dict):
    return list(choices.items())
  return [(choice.get('id') if isinstance(choice, dict) else choice, choice)
          for choice in choices or []]
@

Reading a choice's text needs a function of its own for a duller reason: the
field arrives under two spellings, [[item_body]] from the New Quizzes API and
[[itemBody]] from questions that reached it through the classic conversion.
A choice may also be a bare identifier string rather than an object, which is
what an export written before the ordering mapping was handled correctly
leaves behind (\cref{sec:cleaning-export}); its text is gone for good, and
showing the identifier at least keeps the question's items countable instead
of rendering a run of empty lines.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_choice_text(choice):
  """Returns the display text of one New Quiz choice, as markdown"""
  if not isinstance(choice, dict):
    return html_to_markdown(str(choice))
  return html_to_markdown(choice.get('itemBody')
                          or choice.get('item_body', ''))
@

\paragraph{Multiple choice and multi-answer questions.}
These are the most common question types.
Choices are listed with markers indicating correct answers.
Normalizing first costs nothing for the list this function normally receives,
and means a mapping arriving here by mistake renders the wrong \emph{kind} of
answer instead of aborting the whole view with an [[AttributeError]].

The choices are one half of the question; the other half is the answer key,
and it is written in one of two ways.  The [[view]] command reads a live quiz,
but the same renderer is reached from a file, and the file need not be the one
Canvas wrote: [[--importable]] strips the choice identifiers and puts positions
in their place (\cref{sec:cleaning-export}).  So the same question arrives here
under two descriptions:
\begin{description}
\item[As Canvas delivers it] Every choice carries its [[id]], and
  [[scoring_data.value]] names the correct ones by that identifier---one string
  for [[choice]], a list of strings for [[multi-answer]].
\item[As a cleaned export writes it] The identifiers are gone, each choice
  keeps its [[position]], and the answer key names those positions---the
  integer [[1]], or a list of integers such as [[1, 2, 4]].
\end{description}

Reading only the first of the two had two consequences, and the
harmless-looking one was the worse.  An integer is not a list and not a
string, so the coercion left it alone and [[choice_id in 1]] took down the
whole view with a [[TypeError]]; a list of integers, in contrast, compares
perfectly happily against a set of identifiers and matches nothing, so a
cleaned export rendered every choice as wrong and said nothing about it.

We therefore ask how each reference is written instead of assuming, which is
the reading [[remap_choice_reference]] already applies on the import side: an
integer names a position, a string names an identifier.  Keeping the two apart
is what makes the test honest in both worlds---a position can only ever match a
position---so no identifier can be mistaken for the choice that happens to sit
in that place, and no position for the choice whose identifier happens to be
spelled that way.  The [[bool]] exclusion is the one
[[remap_choice_reference]] needs as well, and for the same reason:
[[isinstance(True, int)]] holds in Python, and a true/false answer that reaches
this function by mistake must not be read as \enquote{position~1}.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_answer_key_references(scoring_data):
  """Returns the positions and the identifiers an answer key names

  A choice or multi-answer answer key names its choices either by
  position (an integer, as a cleaned export writes it) or by identifier
  (a string, as Canvas delivers it); the two are returned separately so
  that neither can match the other.
  """
  value = scoring_data.get('value', [])
  references = value if isinstance(value, list) else [value]

  positions = {reference for reference in references
               if isinstance(reference, int)
               and not isinstance(reference, bool)}
  identifiers = {reference for reference in references
                 if isinstance(reference, str)}

  return positions, identifiers
@

Which position a choice answers to is the second half of that question, and it
has one rule: the [[position]] the choice carries, or else where the choice
sits among its siblings, counting from one.  That rule is not ours to choose
here---it is the one the cleaning writes into the answer key and the one the
import side reads back out of it ([[uuidify_choice_list]])---so the renderer
must count the same way or mark a different line than the export meant.

<<functions>>=
def new_quiz_choice_position(choice, index):
  """Returns the position by which an answer key can name a choice

  A choice carrying its own position field is named by it; any other
  choice is named by its place among the choices, counting from one.
  """
  if isinstance(choice, dict):
    return choice.get('position', index)
  return index
@

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_choice_answers(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data):
  """Format multiple choice or multi-answer question choices"""
  positions, identifiers = new_quiz_answer_key_references(scoring_data)

  for index, (choice_id, choice) in enumerate(new_quiz_choice_pairs(
      interaction_data.get('choices', [])), 1):
    is_correct = (choice_id in identifiers
                  or new_quiz_choice_position(choice, index) in positions)
    marker = "✓" if is_correct else "○"
    lines.append(f"- {marker} {new_quiz_choice_text(choice)}")
@

\paragraph{Ordering questions.}
An ordering question asks the student to arrange the alternatives, so its
answer key is not a selection among the choices but a permutation of them:
[[scoring_data.value]] lists the mapping's keys in the sequence the student is
supposed to produce.

This is why ordering must not be routed to
[[format_new_quiz_choice_answers]], even though the normalization above would
now let it through.  The crash was the smaller of the two objections, and the
one already dealt with; the larger is that a per-line correct/incorrect marker
has nothing to say about this question.  Every choice occurs in the answer key,
so every line
would be marked correct, and the sequence---which is the whole of what the
question asks and the whole of what its answer says---would not appear
anywhere in the output.  Ordering therefore gets a renderer whose output
\emph{is} that sequence: a numbered list, the items in the order the answer
key names.

Two things can go missing without costing the reader the question.  An answer
key naming an identifier the mapping does not carry contributes nothing rather
than raising, and any choice the key never names is listed after the ones it
does, since a question with an item silently omitted is worse than one with an
item out of place.  When no usable answer key survives at all, the items are
still worth showing, under a heading that promises no order rather than one we
cannot substantiate.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_ordering(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data):
  """Format an ordering question as a numbered list in correct order"""
  pairs = new_quiz_choice_pairs(interaction_data.get('choices', {}))
  choices = dict(pairs)
  answer_key = scoring_data.get('value', [])
  if not isinstance(answer_key, list):
    answer_key = []

  ordered_ids = [choice_id for choice_id in answer_key
                 if choice_id in choices]
  ordered_ids += [choice_id for choice_id, _ in pairs
                  if choice_id not in ordered_ids]

  lines.append("**Correct order:**" if answer_key and ordered_ids
               else "**Items to order:**")
  lines.append("")
  for number, choice_id in enumerate(ordered_ids, 1):
    lines.append(f"{number}. {new_quiz_choice_text(choices[choice_id])}")
@

