\paragraph{True/false questions.}
A simplified choice between two options.
Here the [[scoring_data]] value is not a choice identifier but the correct
answer itself---and Canvas delivers it as a JSON boolean, which the JSON
parser turns into Python's [[True]] or [[False]].
Comparing that directly against the string options [['true']] and
[['false']] we display can never match, so no option would ever get the
correct marker.
Rather than assuming one delivery shape, we normalize the value through
[[str(...).lower()]]: a Python boolean stringifies to [['True']]/[['False']]
and lowercases to our option strings, and any string form ([['true']],
[['True']]) normalizes to the same---so both what the API actually sends
and what the code previously assumed are marked correctly.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_true_false(lines, scoring_data):
  """Format true/false question"""
  correct_value = str(scoring_data.get('value', '')).lower()
  for option in ['true', 'false']:
    is_correct = option == correct_value
    marker = "✓" if is_correct else "○"
    lines.append(f"- {marker} {option.capitalize()}")
