\paragraph{Matching questions.}
These pair items from two columns.
The API provides either [[questions]]/[[answers]] or [[stems]]/[[choices]] formats.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_matching(lines, interaction_data, scoring_data):
  """Format matching question"""
  questions = interaction_data.get('questions', []) or interaction_data.get('stems', [])
  correct_matches = scoring_data.get('value', {})

  lines.append("**Match the following:**")
  lines.append("")
  for q in questions:
    q_id = q.get('id', '')
    q_text = html_to_markdown(q.get('item_body', '') or q.get('body', ''))
    match_text = correct_matches.get(q_id, '?') if isinstance(correct_matches, dict) else '?'
    lines.append(f"- {q_text} → {match_text}")
@

\paragraph{Numeric questions.}
These accept exact values, ranges, or values with margins.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_numeric(lines, scoring_data):
  """Format numeric question answer"""
  correct_value = scoring_data.get('value', {})
  if isinstance(correct_value, dict):
    exact = correct_value.get('exact')
    margin = correct_value.get('margin')
    range_min = correct_value.get('start')
    range_max = correct_value.get('end')
    if exact is not None:
      if margin:
        lines.append(f"**Answer:** {exact} (± {margin})")
      else:
        lines.append(f"**Answer:** {exact}")
    elif range_min is not None and range_max is not None:
      lines.append(f"**Answer:** between {range_min} and {range_max}")
  else:
    lines.append(f"**Answer:** {correct_value}")
@

\paragraph{Fill-in-the-blank questions.}
Canvas's documentation and UI call this type \enquote{fill in the blank},
but that is not the name the API emits: the New Quizzes items endpoint
reports the [[interaction_type_slug]] as [[rich-fill-blank]].  An earlier
version of the dispatch matched the assumed name [[fill-in-the-blank]]
instead, so these questions always fell through to the generic fallback
arm and their answers were never rendered.  We match only the real slug;
keeping the assumed name as a second arm would suggest to the reader that
Canvas sometimes sends it, which it never does.

The scoring data is likewise not the mapping from blank id to accepted
answers one might expect.  Each blank carries its \emph{own} scoring
algorithm (for example [[TextEquivalence]] for one blank and
[[TextContainsAnswer]] for another), so [[scoring_data]]'s [[value]] is a
list of per-blank objects, each with a nested [[scoring_data]] holding
that blank's correct answer.  The fill-in-the-blank item in
[[EXAMPLE_NEW_QUIZ_JSON]] below shows the full shape.  We render one
line per blank, keyed by blank id in the same style as
[[format_classic_fill_blanks]], taking the nested [[value]] and falling
back to [[blank_text]] when an algorithm stores the answer only there.
Should Canvas ever hand us a differently shaped value, we skip the
answers rather than crash in the middle of an export.

<<functions>>=
def format_new_quiz_fill_blank(lines, scoring_data):
  """Format fill-in-the-blank question answers"""
  blanks = scoring_data.get('value', [])
  if not isinstance(blanks, list) or not blanks:
    return
  lines.append("**Answers:**")
  for blank in blanks:
    blank_id = blank.get('id', '?')
    blank_scoring = blank.get('scoring_data', {})
    answer = blank_scoring.get('value') or blank_scoring.get('blank_text', '')
    lines.append(f"  [{blank_id}]: {answer}")
