\section{Helper function for HTML to markdown conversion}

Quiz content from Canvas is typically HTML.
Since we're outputting markdown, we need to convert it.

\paragraph{Why pypandoc instead of regex?}
A naive approach would strip HTML tags with a regex like [[re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', html)]].
This fails in many cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item HTML entities like [[&amp;]], [[&lt;]], [[&nbsp;]] remain unconverted
\item Nested tags like [[<p><strong>text</strong></p>]] lose structure
\item Lists, tables, and code blocks lose their formatting
\item Malformed HTML can cause regex to match incorrectly
\end{itemize}

Using [[pypandoc]] (a Python wrapper for Pandoc) handles all these cases correctly.
We delegate to [[content.html_to_markdown]], which all exports share, so that
embedded media in quiz instructions survives the conversion the same way as
on pages (\cref{sec:html-to-markdown}).
We fall back to regex stripping only if Pandoc is unavailable or fails---this
provides degraded but functional output rather than crashing.

<<functions>>=
def html_to_markdown(html_string):
  """Convert HTML to markdown using pypandoc

  Args:
    html_string: String containing HTML content

  Returns:
    Markdown string
  """
  if not html_string:
    return ""

  try:
    markdown = content.html_to_markdown(html_string)
    return markdown.strip()
  except Exception:
    # Fallback: return the string with tags stripped if pandoc fails
    import re
    text = re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', html_string)
    return text.strip()
@


