\subsection{Adding Classic Quiz questions}

Originally, every entry in the [[questions]] array was a question dictionary
and adding questions meant one [[create_question]] call per entry.  Question
groups (\cref{sec:classic-question-groups}) break this one-to-one
correspondence: a group entry wraps a pool of questions from which Canvas
draws a random subset, and the Canvas API requires the group to exist before
its questions can reference it.  We therefore dispatch on the shape of each
entry: a group entry---recognizable by its [[group]] key, which no plain
question dictionary has---creates a question group and its pool, while any
other entry creates a single question as before.

The counts we return only cover individual questions (pool members count one
each); this keeps the \enquote{Added N question(s), M failed} message of all
callers meaningful whether or not groups are involved.

<<functions>>=
def add_classic_questions(quiz, questions):
  """Adds questions to a Classic Quiz.

  Entries in `questions` are either question dictionaries or group
  entries of the form {"group": {...}, "questions": [...]}, which
  create a question group from whose pool Canvas draws questions
  at random.

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object
    questions: List of question or group entry dictionaries

  Returns:
    Tuple of (success_count, failed_count), counting individual
    questions (pool members of a group count one each).
  """
  success = 0
  failed = 0

  for i, entry in enumerate(questions, 1):
    if 'group' in entry:
      group_success, group_failed = add_classic_question_group(
        quiz, entry['group'], entry.get('questions', []))
      success += group_success
      failed += group_failed
    else:
      try:
        quiz.create_question(question=entry)
        print(f"  Added: {entry.get('question_name', f'Question {i}')}")
        success += 1
      except Exception as e:
        print(f"  Failed: {entry.get('question_name', f'Question {i}')} - {e}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        failed += 1

  return success, failed
@

\subsubsection{Creating a question group and its pool}

Canvas creates the group and its questions in separate API calls: first the
group itself (canvasapi's [[create_question_group]]), then one
[[create_question]] call per pool member with [[quiz_group_id]] pointing at
the new group.

The group specification may come straight from an export, which carries
extra keys such as [[id]] and [[position]].  Canvas must not see those---an
[[id]] of a group in another quiz would be meaningless at best---so we
whitelist the three attributes that define a group instead of passing the
specification through verbatim.

If creating the group fails, none of the pool members can reference it, so
we count the whole pool as failed and let the caller continue with the
remaining entries.

<<functions>>=
