\subsection{Exporting New Quiz items}
\label{sec:export-new-quiz-items}

For New Quizzes, we fetch the full item data (not just the summary) and
optionally expand item bank references to include the actual bank questions.

The function first checks the cache for previously fetched items
(\cref{quiz-items-cache} covers the caching details).
If the cache has fresh data, we return it immediately, avoiding the API call.

<<functions>>=
def export_new_quiz_items(quiz, requester, include_banks=True):
  """Exports items from a New Quiz

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object (must have .id and .course attributes)
    requester: Canvas API requester
    include_banks: If True, expand Bank/BankEntry items to include bank questions

  Returns:
    Dictionary with quiz metadata and items
  """
  # Check cache first (only for include_banks=True, the common case)
  if include_banks:
    cached = get_cached_quiz_items(quiz)
    if cached is not None:
      return cached

  import datetime

  course = quiz.course
  assignment_id = quiz.id
  endpoint = f"courses/{course.id}/quizzes/{assignment_id}/items"

  try:
    response = requester.request(
      method='GET',
      endpoint=endpoint,
      _url="new_quizzes"
    )
    raw_items = response.json()
  except Exception as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Failed to fetch New Quiz items: {e}")
    raw_items = []

  items = []
  banks = {}  # bank_id -> bank data (to avoid duplicate fetches)

  for item in raw_items:
    entry_type = item.get('entry_type', 'Item')
    entry = item.get('entry', {})

    # Handle both 'Bank' and 'BankEntry' types - they reference item banks
    if entry_type in ('Bank', 'BankEntry') and include_banks:
      # Check if the entry itself contains a question (embedded bank item)
      nested_entry_type = entry.get('entry_type')
      if nested_entry_type == 'Item' and entry.get('entry'):
        # This BankEntry has an embedded question - extract it
        nested_entry = entry.get('entry', {})
        items.append({
          'position': item.get('position'),
          'id': item.get('id'),
          'entry_type': 'BankEntry (embedded)',
          'points_possible': item.get('points_possible'),
          'entry': nested_entry,
          'source': 'embedded_bank_item'
        })
      else:
        # This is a bank reference - try to fetch the bank's questions
        bank_info = extract_bank_info(item)
        if bank_info:
          bank_id = bank_info.get('bank_id')
          if bank_id and bank_id not in banks:
            # Try to fetch bank items (may fail due to API limitations)
            bank_items = get_bank_items(requester, bank_id)
            banks[bank_id] = {
              'bank_id': bank_id,
              'title': bank_info.get('title', ''),
              'sample_num': bank_info.get('sample_num'),
              'entry_count': bank_info.get('entry_count'),
              'questions': bank_items
            }
          # Include the bank reference with metadata
          items.append({
            'position': item.get('position'),
            'id': item.get('id'),
            'entry_type': entry_type,
            'points_possible': item.get('points_possible'),
            'bank_reference': bank_info
          })
        else:
          # Could not extract bank info, include as-is
          items.append({
            'position': item.get('position'),
            'id': item.get('id'),
            'entry_type': entry_type,
            'points_possible': item.get('points_possible'),
            'entry': entry
          })
    else:
      # Regular question item or stimulus
      items.append({
        'position': item.get('position'),
        'id': item.get('id'),
        'entry_type': entry_type,
        'points_possible': item.get('points_possible'),
        'entry': entry
      })

  export_data = {
    'quiz_type': 'new_quiz',
    'course_id': course.id,
    'assignment_id': assignment_id,
    'export_date': datetime.datetime.now().isoformat(),
    'item_count': len(items),
    'bank_count': len(banks),
    'items': items,
    'banks': banks
  }

  # Cache the result (only for include_banks=True)
  if include_banks:
    cache_quiz_items(quiz, export_data)

  return export_data
@


\subsection{Extracting bank information from Bank/BankEntry items}

Bank and BankEntry items contain information about which item bank to pull
questions from.  The bank ID can be located in different places depending on
the entry type and API version:
\begin{description}
\item[Bank type] The bank information is in the [[entry]] field with [[title]],
  [[entry_count]], etc.  The bank ID may be in [[entry.id]] or we may need to
  look it up separately.
\item[BankEntry type] Similar structure but may include [[properties.sample_num]]
  to indicate how many questions to randomly select from the bank.
\end{description}

<<functions>>=
def extract_bank_info(item):
  """Extracts bank information from a Bank or BankEntry item

  Args:
    item: Quiz item dictionary with entry_type 'Bank' or 'BankEntry'

  Returns:
    Dictionary with bank_id, title, and sample_num, or None
  """
  entry = item.get('entry', {})
  properties = item.get('properties', {})

  # The bank ID might be in different locations depending on API version/type
  # Try multiple possible locations
  bank_id = (
    entry.get('id') or
    entry.get('bank_id') or
    item.get('bank_id') or
    # For some Bank entries, the item ID might reference the bank
    (item.get('id') if item.get('entry_type') == 'Bank' else None)
  )

  # Entry count information (how many questions to pull)
  sample_num = properties.get('sample_num') if properties else None

  if not bank_id and not entry.get('title'):
    return None

  return {
    'bank_id': bank_id,
    'title': entry.get('title', ''),
    'sample_num': sample_num,
    'entry_count': entry.get('entry_count'),
    'item_entry_count': entry.get('item_entry_count'),
    'archived': entry.get('archived', False)
  }
@


\subsection{Exporting Classic Quiz questions}

For Classic Quizzes, we export the full question data including answers.

Questions that belong to a question group must be folded back into the
group-entry format that [[quizzes create]] accepts
(\cref{sec:classic-question-groups}).  Without this, a grouped quiz would
silently export as a flat quiz where every student gets \emph{all} pool
questions instead of a random draw---a particularly insidious loss because
the export still imports without error.  We first convert every question to
a dictionary, then fold grouped questions into group entries.

<<functions>>=
def export_classic_questions(quiz):
  """Exports questions from a Classic Quiz.

  Questions belonging to a question group are folded into group
  entries of the form {"group": {...}, "questions": [...]}, the same
  format that `quizzes create` accepts.

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object

  Returns:
    Dictionary with quiz metadata and questions
  """
  import datetime

  try:
    questions = list(quiz.get_questions())
  except Exception as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Failed to fetch Classic Quiz questions: {e}")
    questions = []

  items = [classic_question_to_dict(q) for q in questions]
  entries = group_classic_questions(quiz, items)

  return {
    'quiz_type': 'classic',
    'quiz_id': quiz.id,
    'course_id': quiz.course_id if hasattr(quiz, 'course_id') else None,
    'export_date': datetime.datetime.now().isoformat(),
    'question_count': len(items),
    'group_count': sum(1 for entry in entries if 'group' in entry),
    'questions': entries
  }
@

The conversion to a dictionary keeps the fields that [[quizzes create]]
understands.  We also record [[quiz_group_id]] so the grouping step below can
recover the groups; that step removes it again, since the create side
receives the group membership from the nesting instead.

<<functions>>=
def classic_question_to_dict(q):
  """Converts a canvasapi QuizQuestion to an exportable dictionary."""
  item = {
    'id': q.id,
    'position': getattr(q, 'position', None),
    'quiz_group_id': getattr(q, 'quiz_group_id', None),
    'question_name': getattr(q, 'question_name', ''),
    'question_text': getattr(q, 'question_text', ''),
    'question_type': getattr(q, 'question_type', ''),
    'points_possible': getattr(q, 'points_possible', 0),
    'answers': getattr(q, 'answers', [])
  }
  # Include additional fields if present
  for field in ['correct_comments', 'incorrect_comments', 'neutral_comments',
                'matching_answer_incorrect_matches', 'formulas', 'variables']:
    if hasattr(q, field):
      item[field] = getattr(q, field)
  return item
@

\subsubsection{Folding grouped questions into group entries}

The Canvas API has no endpoint to list a quiz's question groups; a group can
only be fetched by id.  We therefore recover the groups from the questions
themselves: each question carries a [[quiz_group_id]], and the distinct ids
tell us which groups to fetch.

Ordering needs care.  One might expect to sort by the questions' and the
group's [[position]] attributes, but in practice (verified against KTH
Canvas, July 2026) the questions index returns \emph{no} positions at all:
every question's [[position]] is [[None]], and only the group object carries
one.  A position-based sort therefore cannot interleave the group with the
ungrouped questions.  What the API does provide reliably is list order:
[[get_questions]] returns the questions in quiz order, with pool members in
their group's slot.  So we walk the questions in API order and emit each
group entry at its \emph{first} member's slot; any later members---adjacent
in every quiz we have seen, but nothing forces them to be---are folded into
that same entry.

If fetching a group fails, we still emit the group entry with only its
[[id]]: flattening the pool into ordinary questions would silently change
what students see, whereas an entry without [[pick_count]] fails loudly on
re-import.

<<functions>>=
def group_classic_questions(quiz, items):
  """Folds questions sharing a quiz_group_id into group entries.

  Args:
    quiz: Quiz object (used to fetch the question groups)
    items: List of question dictionaries carrying quiz_group_id

  Returns:
    List of entries in quiz order: plain question dictionaries and
    {"group": {...}, "questions": [...]} group entries.
  """
  entries = []
  pools = {}  # group id -> pool list, shared with the group's entry

  for item in items:
    group_id = item.pop('quiz_group_id', None)
    if group_id is None:
      entries.append(item)
    elif group_id in pools:
      pools[group_id].append(item)
    else:
      pools[group_id] = [item]
      entries.append({'group': fetch_question_group_info(quiz, group_id),
                      'questions': pools[group_id]})

  <<sort each pool by position>>
  return entries
@

Note that each group entry and [[pools]] share the same list object, so
members found later in the walk still end up inside the already-emitted
entry.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_question_group_info(quiz, group_id):
  """Fetches a question group's defining attributes, keeping its id."""
  group_info = {'id': group_id}
  try:
    group = quiz.get_quiz_group(group_id)
    group_info['name'] = getattr(group, 'name', None)
    group_info['pick_count'] = getattr(group, 'pick_count', None)
    group_info['question_points'] = getattr(group, 'question_points', None)
  except Exception as e:
    canvaslms.cli.warn(f"Failed to fetch question group {group_id}: {e}")
  return group_info
@

Within a pool we do sort by [[position]] when Canvas provides one (the
positions of pool members, when present, are relative to the group, so they
are comparable to each other).  A sort key must never compare [[None]] with
[[None]]---Python raises [[TypeError]] for that---so we map missing
positions to a last-sorting key with a comparable second component; the
stable sort then preserves API order among position-less members.

<<sort each pool by position>>=
for pool in pools.values():
  pool.sort(key=lambda q: position_sort_key(q.get('position')))
@

<<functions>>=
def position_sort_key(position):
  """Sort key placing None positions last, preserving order among them."""
  return (position is None, position if position is not None else 0)
@

\subsubsection{Verifying the folding of grouped questions}

Let's verify the folding on the tricky case that motivated the design: pool
members that are \emph{not} adjacent in the API's question list, and whose
positions are relative to the group.  The group entry must appear at its
first member's slot in the walk order, later members must fold into the
same entry (sorted by their group-relative positions), and
[[quiz_group_id]] must not leak into the export.

Note that [[MagicMock(name=...)]] sets the mock's \emph{mock name}, not a
[[name]] attribute, so we assign the group's [[name]] after construction.

<<test functions>>=
def test_group_classic_questions_folds_nonadjacent():
  group = MagicMock(pick_count=1, question_points=2, position=2)
  group.name = 'parallel forms'
  quiz = MagicMock()
  quiz.get_quiz_group.return_value = group

  items = [
    {'id': 1, 'position': 1, 'quiz_group_id': None, 'question_name': 'Q1'},
    {'id': 2, 'position': 2, 'quiz_group_id': 5, 'question_name': 'Form B'},
    {'id': 3, 'position': 3, 'quiz_group_id': None, 'question_name': 'Q3'},
    {'id': 4, 'position': 1, 'quiz_group_id': 5, 'question_name': 'Form A'},
  ]

  entries = quizzes.group_classic_questions(quiz, items)

  quiz.get_quiz_group.assert_called_once_with(5)
  names = [entry.get('question_name', 'group') for entry in entries]
  assert names == ['Q1', 'group', 'Q3']

  group_entry = entries[1]
  assert group_entry['group'] == {'id': 5, 'name': 'parallel forms',
                                  'pick_count': 1, 'question_points': 2}
  pool_names = [q['question_name'] for q in group_entry['questions']]
  assert pool_names == ['Form A', 'Form B']

  for entry in entries:
    for question in [entry] if 'group' not in entry else entry['questions']:
      assert 'quiz_group_id' not in question
@

When the group cannot be fetched, the entry must keep the pool grouped
(with only the group [[id]]) rather than silently flattening it into
